Control Theory: Empirical vs Analytical Methods
• Analytical method: Systematic procedures for designing systems and reducing the likelihood of designing flawed or disastrous systems.
• Modeling, setting up mathematical equations, analysis, and design.
• Do we need repeated experimentation for analytical methods, similar to empirical ones?
• Yes, but using numerical simulations before the actual application.
• With the adequately chosen model, the resemblance of system performance with that predicted by analytical designs or computer simulations.
• Improvement in performance of physical systems by fine adjustments or tunings (discrepancies due to unavoidable inaccuracy in modeling).
• Lengthy testing before the actual operation or mass production.
• Experimentation needed to set up models and experience needed to improve the performance.
