• Plant: Physical layout of a plant, physical models
• Knowledge of the basic energy balance, mass balance, and material flows in the system
• Objectives of control design:
▷ What does one want to achieve? ▷ What variables need to be controlled to achieve these objectives? ▷ What level of performance is necessary?
• Sensors: Eye of the controller
• “If you can measure it, you can control it”
• Actuators: Ability to affect or actuate the system to move the process from the current state to a desired state
Shashi Ranjan Kumar (IITB) AE 695 : State Space Methods for Flight Vehicles Motivation for Feedback Control Lecture 1 15 / 28


Control Theory: Success in Control Engineering
• Communication systems: Interconnecting sensors to actuators
• Communication delay in transmission: failure of control
• Computational delays, numerical precision, ease of design and implementation
• Architectures and interfacing: what to connect to what?
• Centralized control: Complexity, cost, time constraints in computation, maintainability, reliability
• Partition of control problem into manageable sub-systems
• Algorithms: Connect the sensors to the actuators (Heart of control engineering)
• Example: Eye-hand coordination for tennis player
• Disturbances and Uncertainty: Significant impact on the system performance
• Desired level of performance by using relatively simple models
• Homogeneity: Interconnected systems are only as good as their weakest element.
• Focus on the weakest component to get the best return for a given level of investment.
